**Kafka Event Store Implementation:**

```csharp
public class KafkaEventStore : IEventStore
{
    private readonly IProducer<string, byte[]> _producer;
    private readonly IConsumer<string, byte[]> _consumer;

    public async Task AppendEventsAsync(
        Guid aggregateId,
        IEnumerable<IDomainEvent> events,
        long expectedVersion)
    {
        var topic = $"claim-events";

        foreach (var @event in events)
        {
            var message = new Message<string, byte[]>
            {
                Key = aggregateId.ToString(),  // Partition by aggregate ID
                Value = JsonSerializer.SerializeToUtf8Bytes(@event),
                Headers = new Headers
                {
                    { "EventType", Encoding.UTF8.GetBytes(@event.GetType().Name) },
                    { "EventVersion", BitConverter.GetBytes(@event.Version) },
                    { "Timestamp", BitConverter.GetBytes(DateTime.UtcNow.Ticks) }
                }
            };

            await _producer.ProduceAsync(topic, message);
        }
    }

    public async Task<IEnumerable<IDomainEvent>> LoadEventsAsync(
        Guid aggregateId)
    {
        // Note: Kafka not optimized for loading by aggregate ID
        // Consider maintaining separate store (PostgreSQL) for aggregate loading
        // Use Kafka for event streaming and cross-system integration

        throw new NotSupportedException(
            "Loading aggregate history from Kafka not recommended. " +
            "Use PostgreSQL for aggregate persistence, Kafka for event streaming.");
    }
}
```

**Recommendation**: Use **PostgreSQL for event store** + **Kafka for integration events**. This hybrid approach leverages PostgreSQL for aggregate loading and Kafka for cross-system event streaming.

